{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nRemove both duplicated YAML CAS loops only after [rebar:953a-d616-52d3-4efe] and [rebar:2bc9-75aa-a08a-478a] have independently proven the core delivery contracts. The reconciler loop carries inbound events that exist nowhere else and must fail loud; the canary loop re-pushes already-committed state but currently exits nonzero on persistent failure, so that strict disposition must remain intact.\n\nThis change must preserve continuity for every currently deployed workflow and copied client template: the old YAML vocabulary disappears only in the same change that wires the equivalent core functionality and exactly two executable smoke tests, one for `reconcile-bridge.yml` and one for `reconcile-bridge-canary.yml`.\n\n## Approach\n\n- Replace the reconciler commit-back block in `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml`, after its existing `cd .tickets-tracker`, with the complete adapter call:\n  `REBAR_SYNC_PUSH=always python -m rebar._store.push commit-and-push --tracker . --message \"chore: sync events from rebar reconciler [run ${{ github.run_id }}]\" --strict --author-name \"$BRIDGE_BOT_NAME\" --author-email \"$BRIDGE_BOT_EMAIL\"`.\n- Remove the reconciler workflow's sole `raw-git-ok` marker.\n- Replace the flush loop in `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge-canary.yml`, after its existing `cd .tickets-tracker`, with the complete push-only call:\n  `REBAR_SYNC_PUSH=always python -m rebar._store.push push --tracker . --strict`.\n- Preserve the reconciler's exact subject and bot identity, and preserve both workflows' current red-on-merge-conflict, non-retriable-decline, and retry-exhaustion disposition through `--strict`.\n- Update both workflow standing-decision blocks, the obsolete `scripts/canary_bridge.py` statement, and every stale test rationale. In `tests/unit/test_canary_bridge_wiring.py`, update the module rationale, `test_flush_cas_loop_stays_in_yaml_verbatim_shape`, and the sibling `test_no_logic_bearing_rebar_calls_left_in_yaml` rationale that still says ticket `4c4f` deliberately keeps the flush loop in YAML.\n- Replace the anti-migration assertion with the continuity contract: exactly one canary flush step invokes push-only `python -m rebar._store.push push --tracker . --strict`, pins `REBAR_SYNC_PUSH=always`, contains no inline CAS loop/raw git, and preserves persistent-failure red behavior.\n- Retire or rewrite `tests/interfaces/store/test_reconcile_workflow_no_shallow_rebase.py::test_reconcile_uses_merge_not_rebase`, whose per-workflow assertion expects the deleted `git merge --no-edit origin/tickets` line, and `tests/unit/workflow/test_reconcile_workflow_lint.py::test_reconcile_workflows_provision_the_ours_driver_once`, whose `_first_merge_index(canary)` requirement expects a YAML-local merge. Re-derive the full reference set by grepping the exact deleted commands `git merge --no-edit origin/tickets` and `git push origin HEAD:tickets` across `tests/`.\n- Replace `tests/scripts/test_reconcile_bridge_push_retry.py`'s extracted-loop test with thin executable tests of the exact workflow blocks against real temporary repositories.\n- Record the copied-template shape change in `CHANGELOG.md`; update `docs/jira-sync-setup.md` with the opt-in refresh action for existing copied loops and the no-cleanup rollback to the prior inline block.\n- After landing, comment on `4c4f-e554-849a-4dfd` with the overturn rationale and green evidence.\n\n## Testing\n\nUse executable workflow-block smoke tests, not source-text assertions alone.\n\nRED-first oracle:\n- reconciler workflow starts with a dirty tracker and must commit/push with the workflow-supplied identity;\n- a rejected reconciler push exits nonzero, proving `--strict` is wired;\n- project config `sync.push=off` is overridden by the call-site `REBAR_SYNC_PUSH=always`;\n- canary workflow starts locally ahead, a persistent rejection exits nonzero, and the commit remains pending;\n- each exact block invokes the named private module CLI once with every required argument; subprocess execution proves argparse accepts the workflow shape and reaches the intended core action;\n- reconciler message and identity are preserved; raw git mutation is absent;\n- the former `test_flush_cas_loop_stays_in_yaml_verbatim_shape` is RED after loop removal until rewritten to assert the thin push-only strict canary step, and both the file-level and sibling-test rationales are updated from \"never migrate\" to the dependency-proven core ownership decision;\n- the two merge-command-dependent workflow tests are RED after loop removal until rewritten to assert the new core-owned boundary rather than a YAML-local implementation;\n- mutation removing `--strict`, the synchronous override, an authorship flag, `--tracker`, `--message`, or changing canary to commit-and-push makes the corresponding smoke test RED.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] Both inline YAML CAS loops are absent and each target step invokes exactly one named `python -m rebar._store.push` action with the complete required CLI arguments.\n- [x] Reconciler and canary delivery are both strict and synchronous, preserving each current workflow's red-on-persistent-failure contract while library/MCP callers retain the best-effort default.\n- [x] Reconciler commits retain the configured `BRIDGE_BOT_NAME` / `BRIDGE_BOT_EMAIL` identity and the exact subject `chore: sync events from rebar reconciler [run ${{ github.run_id }}]`.\n- [x] The reconciler's sole `raw-git-ok` marker is removed and `scripts/check_raw_git_writes.py` passes without an exemption.\n- [x] Both standing-decision blocks plus module/test rationales document the reversal; specifically `tests/unit/test_canary_bridge_wiring.py` updates both `test_flush_cas_loop_stays_in_yaml_verbatim_shape` and `test_no_logic_bearing_rebar_calls_left_in_yaml` so they pin exactly one thin push-only strict flush step with synchronous override, no raw git/CAS loop, and preserved red failure disposition.\n- [x] `tests/scripts/test_reconcile_bridge_push_retry.py` no longer extracts or executes the removed reconciler YAML block; its anti-migration rationale is retired, and the in-process `push.py` contention suite owns rejection→fetch→merge→re-push coverage.\n- [x] `tests/interfaces/store/test_reconcile_workflow_no_shallow_rebase.py` and `tests/unit/workflow/test_reconcile_workflow_lint.py` no longer require YAML-local merge commands removed with the CAS loops; they continue to protect the applicable checkout/merge-driver contracts at the new core-owned boundary.\n- [x] Exactly two executable workflow smoke tests pin the complete `commit-and-push --tracker . --message ... --strict --author-name ... --author-email ...` reconciler invocation and complete push-only `push --tracker . --strict` canary invocation; both preserve the old outcomes and assert the direct module invocation has no git-output pipe into `tee` or another consumer.\n- [x] `CHANGELOG.md` identifies `reconcile-bridge-canary.yml` as a client-copied template; `docs/jira-sync-setup.md` gives existing copies the opt-in refresh action, explains the immediate-re-push timing change, and documents both no-cleanup rollbacks: restore the prior canary inline loop, or switch the reconciler `commit-and-push --strict` call site back to best-effort without rewriting store state.\n- [x] A comment on `4c4f-e554-849a-4dfd` records the overturned decision and landed evidence.\n- [x] [operator-attested] Focused workflow tests, `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and `make test` pass.\n\n## Compatibility and rollback\n\nExisting client copies do not update automatically, so the changelog provides the exact refresh action. Revert this cutover to restore both inline loops while leaving the unused core capabilities harmless. The canary currently has five attempts plus exponential sleep. The core preserves five attempts and terminal nonzero behavior but intentionally re-pushes immediately after a clean fetch+merge: HEAD is then a valid fast-forward and sleeping only gives a competitor time to invalidate it, as observed in run 30585738860. `CHANGELOG.md` and `docs/jira-sync-setup.md` name this timing change and the rollback to the prior inline loop. No CLI, MCP, library, persisted-schema, or event-vocabulary change occurs."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786170642172774001,"uuid":"c95f915f-f99b-4c39-9916-ed994434ba8a"}